summary:
- topic: Grievance Denial
  text: Following an executive session, the board voted to deny a grievance and directed
    the superintendent to provide written notice to the grievant and union. The vote
    was 5 in favor and 2 opposed.
- topic: Student and Staff Recognitions
  text: The board celebrated the Boys Outdoor Track and Field team for winning states
    and individual music students for Allstate and regional honors. Additionally,
    Mr. CraigSkeffington was recognized for his induction into the MaineMusicEducatorsAssociation
    Hall of Fame, with heartfelt tributes from former students and the Board Chair.
- topic: SPHSRoboticsTeam Grant
  text: The SPHSRoboticsTeam received a $5,000 grant from ConsolidatedCommunications,
    presented by a team mentor and representative, to support training and building
    materials for their upcoming season.
- topic: Community Partnerships Report
  text: Gretchen McCloy and Brenna Latham presented an extensive report on community
    engagement, highlighting 498 background-checked volunteers, significant donations
    from organizations like the Locker Project and Soles for Soles, and various career
    exploration programs for students. They emphasized the importance of these partnerships
    in enriching student experiences and supporting school needs.
- topic: Federal Title Funding Update
  text: Dr. Prince provided an update on federal Title Funding (ESEA) for FY25, noting
    a significant decrease of almost $150,000, primarily in Title I. These funds support
    students from lower socioeconomic backgrounds, professional development, multilingual
    students, and student support/enrichment, with Kaler and Skillin Elementary Schools
    being major recipients.
- topic: Facilities Bond Referendum Discussion
  text: The superintendent presented recommendations for a potential $17 million facilities
    bond referendum, covering elementary school security, deferred maintenance (roofs,
    windows, SPHS issues), and high school athletic facilities, including a proposal
    for recyclable artificial turf with organic infill. Board members raised concerns
    about the environmental impact and true recyclability of artificial turf.
- topic: Resolution for Separate Bond Referendums
  text: 'The board approved an amended resolution directing administration to prepare
    formal proposals for three separate bond referendums: one for school safety, one
    for deferred maintenance, and one for athletic and stadium facilities. This allows
    for independent consideration and voting on each component, with a total cost
    not to exceed $17 million.'
